Now, the setInterval only includes the handleProgressBar function, which will be called every second to update the progress bar based on the isGenerating state and the elapsed time since the last generation. The generateImage function can be invoked separately as needed without being triggered by the interval.

you cannot use funct by funct evaluations in intervals in javascript or what? as: "setInterval(handleProgressBar - generateImage) / 1000;" progbar will be based on genimg.

Apologies for the confusion. You’re correct that you can’t directly use function evaluations in setInterval in JavaScript. The setInterval function requires a reference to a single function to be called at the specified interval.
If you want the handleProgressBar function to be called periodically based on the processing of the generateImage function, you can modify the code as follows:

let startTime = null;
let previousGenerationTime = null;
let isFirstGeneration = true;
function generateImage() {
isGenerating = true;
// Rest of the generateImage logic
isGenerating = false;
}
function handleProgressBar() {
const progressBarFilled = document.querySelector(‘.progress-bar-filled’);
if (isGenerating || isFirstGeneration) {
if (isFirstGeneration) {
startTime = Date.now();
isFirstGeneration = false;
progressBarFilled.innerText = ‘loading’;
} else {
const currentTime = Date.now();
const secondsPassed = Math.floor((currentTime - previousGenerationTime) / 1000);
progressBarFilled.innerText = done: ${secondsPassed} sec;
}
previousGenerationTime = Date.now();
} else {
progressBarFilled.innerText = ‘’;
}
}
// Call the handleProgressBar function periodically and trigger the generateImage function in that interval
setInterval(() => {
generateImage();
handleProgressBar();
}, 1000);

In this updated code, the generateImage function is called within the interval, followed by the handleProgressBar function. This way, the handleProgressBar function will be called periodically based on the processing of the generateImage function.

